<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Li Keyong</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">856–908</span> · <a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Li Keyong was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shatuo">Shatuo</a> Turkic military leader in the last decades of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tang-dynasty">Tang dynasty</a>. His cavalry drove <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huang-chao">Huang Chao</a>'s rebels from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/changan">Chang'an</a> in 883, and the Tang made him Prince of Jin, ruling from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taiyuan">Taiyuan</a>. He was the great rival of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhu-wen">Zhu Wen</a>, founder of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/later-liang">Later Liang</a>, and his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/li-cunxu">Li Cunxu</a> destroyed the Liang and founded the Later Tang in 923.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/li-keyong.jpg" alt="Li Keyong" width="320" height="670" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown artistUnknown artist · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Li_Keyong.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
